title: The full adder in VHDL
mode: vhdl
chapter: Arithmetic
check: behaves
---- intro
The full adder as text. `s` is 1 when an odd number of the three inputs are 1, which you wrote in the parity lesson. `cout` is 1 when at least two of them are, which is the majority vote you drew.

Big circuits are made of small ones you already know.
---- task
Write the full adder: `s` and `cout` from `a`, `b` and `cin`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity full_adder is
  port(
    a    : in  std_logic;
    b    : in  std_logic;
    cin  : in  std_logic;
    s    : out std_logic;
    cout : out std_logic
  );
end full_adder;

architecture rtl of full_adder is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity full_adder is
  port(
    a    : in  std_logic;
    b    : in  std_logic;
    cin  : in  std_logic;
    s    : out std_logic;
    cout : out std_logic
  );
end full_adder;

architecture rtl of full_adder is
begin
    s    <= a xor b xor cin;
    cout <= (a and b) or (a and cin) or (b and cin);
end rtl;
---- hint1
`s` is the parity of the three inputs; `cout` is their majority.
---- hint2
`s <= a xor b xor cin;` and `cout <= (a and b) or (a and cin) or (b and cin);`
---- solution
```
s    <= a xor b xor cin;
cout <= (a and b) or (a and cin) or (b and cin);
```
---- done
Two lines, and you have the heart of a processor's arithmetic. Soon VHDL will add whole numbers for you with `+`.
